>> 13 How do I say "Can we have the bill, please?" in Portuguese?
[completed 4s run=cmwvor63v002g07p9z4tcg3w6] In Portuguese (European Portuguese, since you're in Lisbon):

**"A conta, por favor."** — "The bill, please."

Or more explicitly:

**"Poderíamos ter a conta, por favor?"** — "Could we have the bill, please?"

A couple of practical notes for Portugal:
- To get someone's attention, you can simply say **"Desconsa!"**... actually, the common way is to catch the waiter's eye and say **"Por favor"** or **"Ó senhor / ó senhora"** is a bit old-fashioned — a friendly **"Desculpe"** (excuse me) works well.
- "Desconsa" isn't a word — ignore that slip; the practical phrase is: raise a hand slightly and say **"A conta, por favor."**

**Bonus:** in Portugal the bill doesn't come automatically — you usually have to ask, so this is a very useful phrase! 💶

Pronunciation tip: "A conta, por favor" ≈ "ah KON-ta, poor fa-VOR".
